<body>
<p class="p1"><span class="s1"></span><br></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">title: "Why 'Free' Contractor Quote Sites Cost You More Than You Think" description: "HomeAdvisor, Thumbtack, and Porch are free to use—but you pay in other ways. Here's how lead-gen sites affect your project costs and privacy." date: 2024-02-03 author: Ungouge Team keywords: ["homeadvisor cost", "thumbtack pricing", "free contractor quotes", "lead generation sites", "contractor marketplace problems", "alternative to homeadvisor"]</span></p>
<p class="p1"><span class="s1"></span><br></p>
<h1 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 16.1px 0.0px; font: 24.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #138756; -webkit-text-stroke: #138756"><span class="s1"><b>Why "Free" Contractor Quote Sites Cost You More Than You Think</b></span></h1>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">HomeAdvisor is free. Thumbtack is free. Porch, Modernize, and a dozen others—all free for homeowners.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">So what's the catch?</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">The catch is simple: <b>If you're not paying for the product, you are the product.</b></span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">These platforms don't make money from you directly. They make money by selling your project details to contractors. And that business model creates problems that can cost you thousands—even if you never pay the platform a dime.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Let's break down the real cost of "free" contractor marketplaces.</span></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>The Business Model: You're the Commodity</b></span></h2>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Here's how it actually works:</span></p>
<ol class="ol1">
  <li class="li5"><span class="s2"><b></b></span><span class="s1"><b>You fill out a form</b> with your project details (what you need, budget range, timeline, contact info)</span></li>
  <li class="li5"><span class="s2"><b></b></span><span class="s1"><b>The platform sells your information</b> to 3-6 contractors who pay $15-$60 per lead</span></li>
  <li class="li5"><span class="s2"><b></b></span><span class="s1"><b>Contractors call/email/text you</b> trying to win your business</span></li>
  <li class="li5"><span class="s2"><b></b></span><span class="s1"><b>You pick one</b> (or give up from spam exhaustion)</span></li>
</ol>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Seems harmless, right? Wrong.</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>Problem #1: You Get Bombarded with Sales Calls</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">The moment you submit your project, your phone becomes a weapon against you.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Real user experience (from Reddit r/HomeImprovement):</b></span></p>
<blockquote style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 12.0px 0.0px; font: 12.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #535353; -webkit-text-stroke: #535353"><span class="s1">"I used HomeAdvisor to find a deck builder. Within 20 minutes I had 7 calls, 11 texts, and 4 emails. For the next two weeks my phone wouldn't stop ringing. I eventually just changed my number."</span></blockquote>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Why does this happen?</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Because contractors <b>pay for your contact info</b> whether you hire them or not. They've got skin in the game, and they're going to extract every ounce of value from that lead—meaning aggressive follow-up.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>What it costs you:</b> - Time dealing with unwanted calls - Stress and decision fatigue - Potential missed calls from legitimate contacts (buried in spam) - In extreme cases: changing your phone number ($$$)</span></p>
<p class="p1"><span class="s1"></span><br></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>Problem #2: Contractors Pad Quotes to Cover Lead Costs</b></span></h2>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Here's the economics contractors face:</span></p>
<ul class="ul1">
  <li class="li5"><span class="s2"><b></b></span><span class="s1"><b>Average cost per lead on HomeAdvisor:</b> $30-$60</span></li>
  <li class="li5"><span class="s2"><b></b></span><span class="s1"><b>Lead-to-hire conversion rate:</b> ~5-10% (optimistic)</span></li>
  <li class="li5"><span class="s2"><b></b></span><span class="s1"><b>Meaning:</b> A contractor needs to spend $300-$1,200 in lead costs to land one job</span></li>
</ul>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Where do you think that money comes from?</b></span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">It gets baked into the quotes they give you.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">If a contractor knows they need to burn through $600 in lead costs to close a job, they're adding that to your project—either as direct markup or by inflating material costs, labor hours, or contingency percentages.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Example math:</b> - Fair market price for kitchen remodel: $28,000 - Contractor's HomeAdvisor lead costs (amortized): $1,200 - Quote you actually get: $30,500</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">You're paying for the platform's business model even though you never got charged directly.</span></p>
<p class="p1"><span class="s1"></span><br></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>Problem #3: Quality Isn't Guaranteed (Despite the Promises)</b></span></h2>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">HomeAdvisor, Thumbtack, and others advertise "pre-screened" or "top-rated" contractors.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">What does that actually mean?</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>HomeAdvisor's screening (per their own disclosures):</b> - Background check (if the contractor opts in) - License verification (where applicable) - Insurance verification (where applicable)</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>What it doesn't include:</b> - Quality of work assessment - Customer satisfaction enforcement - Past project reviews (outside their platform) - Financial stability checks</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>The problem:</b><br>
A contractor can be "HomeAdvisor Certified" and still: - Consistently run over budget - Miss deadlines - Do shoddy work that needs redoing - Ghost customers after getting deposits</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">The "screening" is bare minimum and mostly focused on legal/insurance compliance—not quality.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>From Trustpilot reviews of HomeAdvisor (2.0/5 stars):</b></span></p>
<blockquote style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 12.0px 0.0px; font: 12.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #535353; -webkit-text-stroke: #535353"><span class="s1">"The 'pro' they connected me with asked for 50% upfront, did two days of work, then disappeared. HomeAdvisor wouldn't refund my lead because I 'agreed to hire' someone from their platform."</span></blockquote>
<p class="p1"><span class="s1"></span><br></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>Problem #4: Reviews Are Gamed</b></span></h2>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">On these platforms, contractors can:</span></p>
<ol class="ol1">
  <li class="li5"><span class="s2"><b></b></span><span class="s1"><b>Request reviews only from happy customers</b> (and ignore unhappy ones)</span></li>
  <li class="li5"><span class="s2"><b></b></span><span class="s1"><b>Bury bad reviews</b> by generating volume from easy/small jobs</span></li>
  <li class="li5"><span class="s2"><b></b></span><span class="s1"><b>Respond to negative reviews with rebuttals</b> that make the customer look unreasonable</span></li>
  <li class="li5"><span class="s2"><b></b></span><span class="s1"><b>Pay for premium placement</b> which looks like "top-rated" to consumers</span></li>
</ol>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>The result:</b> A contractor with a 4.7-star rating might be average at best.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Compare this to: - <b>Google Reviews:</b> Harder to game because they're public and cross-referenced - <b>Better Business Bureau:</b> Formal complaint process with resolution tracking - <b>Local references:</b> Real humans you can call and ask direct questions</span></p>
<p class="p1"><span class="s1"></span><br></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>Problem #5: Your Data Gets Sold (and Resold)</b></span></h2>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">When you submit your info to HomeAdvisor, you agree to their Terms of Service.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Buried in the fine print:</b> - They can share your information with "service providers, vendors, and partners" - They can use your data for marketing purposes - Third-party contractors can contact you for "related services"</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>What this means in practice:</b> - You wanted a bathroom quote, now you're getting HVAC spam - Your info enters marketing databases - You get retargeting ads for months - Other lead-gen companies buy your data from partner networks</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>You wanted one contractor. You got a permanent spot on a dozen marketing lists.</b></span></p>
<p class="p1"><span class="s1"></span><br></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>Problem #6: No Price Transparency or Quote Validation</b></span></h2>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Here's what these platforms <b>don't</b> do:</span></p>
<ul class="ul1">
  <li class="li5"><span class="s2"></span><span class="s1">Tell you if a quote is fair</span></li>
  <li class="li5"><span class="s2"></span><span class="s1">Break down where markup is coming from</span></li>
  <li class="li5"><span class="s2"></span><span class="s1">Compare line items to market rates</span></li>
  <li class="li5"><span class="s2"></span><span class="s1">Flag suspiciously vague charges</span></li>
  <li class="li5"><span class="s2"></span><span class="s1">Give you negotiation leverage</span></li>
</ul>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">They connect you with contractors. That's it.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>You're on your own to figure out if the $35,000 kitchen quote is reasonable or inflated by 30%.</b></span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">And if you're already paying for leads, do you think contractors are incentivized to give you the lowest price? Or the highest price they think you'll accept?</span></p>
<p class="p1"><span class="s1"></span><br></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>The Alternative: Quote Analysis, Not Quote Generation</b></span></h2>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Instead of using lead-gen platforms that sell your data and inflate costs, consider a different approach:</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>1. Find contractors yourself</b> - Google reviews - Local referrals from neighbors - BBB-accredited businesses - Social media groups (NextDoor, local Facebook groups)</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>2. Get quotes directly</b> (no middleman padding)</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>3. Use a quote analysis tool</b> (like Ungouge) to verify pricing</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Why this works:</b> - No lead costs baked into quotes - No spam from contractors you didn't ask for - No data sales to marketing networks - <b>Contractors compete on quality and price, not who paid for your contact info</b></span></p>
<p class="p1"><span class="s1"></span><br></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>What About the "Guarantees"?</b></span></h2>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">HomeAdvisor offers a "Happiness Guarantee" up to $2,500.<br>
Thumbtack has a similar policy.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Sounds great, right?</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>The fine print:</b> - You must report issues within specific timeframes (often 30-60 days) - The work must have been booked <i>and paid</i> through the platform - They can deny claims if the contractor disputes the complaint - It's a reimbursement, not a prevention tool</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Translation:</b> It's damage control, not quality assurance.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Wouldn't you rather: - Hire a quality contractor from the start? - Pay fair market rates? - Not deal with spam and data sales?</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Prevention &gt; insurance.</span></p>
<p class="p1"><span class="s1"></span><br></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>The Bottom Line</b></span></h2>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">"Free" contractor quote sites aren't actually free. You pay with:</span></p>
<ol class="ol1">
  <li class="li5"><span class="s2"><b></b></span><span class="s1"><b>Your privacy</b> (data sold to marketing networks)</span></li>
  <li class="li5"><span class="s2"><b></b></span><span class="s1"><b>Your time</b> (spam calls, unwanted follow-ups)</span></li>
  <li class="li5"><span class="s2"><b></b></span><span class="s1"><b>Your money</b> (quotes inflated to cover lead costs)</span></li>
  <li class="li5"><span class="s2"><b></b></span><span class="s1"><b>Your peace of mind</b> (no real quality guarantees)</span></li>
</ol>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">There's a better way: <b>Get quotes directly, analyze them with real data, hire with confidence.</b></span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">No middleman. No spam. No games.</span></p>
<p class="p1"><span class="s1"></span><br></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>How Ungouge Is Different</b></span></h2>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">We don't sell leads. We don't sell your data. We don't spam you.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>What we do:</b> - You upload your contractor quote - We compare it to real market data - We show you where pricing might be inflated - <b>You</b> decide what to do with that information</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>We work for you, not contractors.</b></span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Our business model is simple: homeowners pay a small fee for analysis. Contractors never see your info unless you choose to contact them directly.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">No spam. No data sales. No BS.</span></p>
<p class="p8"><span class="s3"><b>Analyze Your Quote Now →</b></span></p>
